  "cause": "A new entry, and the cause is a land-use decision multiplied by a weather pattern. **Wildfires have burned some 1.9 million hectares across Indonesia since the start of the year, according to the open-access satellite platform Nusantara Atlas — an area roughly the size of New Jersey, or the whole landmass of Fiji — with one million hectares thought to have burned in September alone** (BBC, citing Nusantara Atlas). **Smouldering forest, swamp and peatland have filled the air with microparticles and carbon monoxide, and the resulting haze has blanketed Indonesia and drifted as far as Singapore, Malaysia and the Philippines. As of mid-October the health ministry had recorded more than 277,000 cases of chronic cough and acute respiratory infection, of which more than 62,000 were in infants and children under five** (BBC; Indonesian health ministry). **A \"Super El Niño\" has fuelled months of burning** (BBC). **What the numbers describe is a distribution of harm, and the people bearing it say so plainly.** **In Palangkaraya, Central Kalimantan, the sky has turned yellow; a mother of three describes her baby's chest rattling at night, and a hospitalisation for bronchopneumonia at six weeks old; the head of the city's health office says residents are breathing carbon dioxide and pollutants rather than oxygen, and that the brain does not get enough of it. Official advice is to stay indoors — advice the same mother calls unrealistic, because she still has to buy vegetables, stock food and get school supplies. Haze and smoke from peat fires carry microparticles that can penetrate deep into the lungs and, in some cases, enter the bloodstream, causing inflammation, chronic lung diseases and an increased risk of premature death** (BBC). **The political response has begun: protests in Indonesia are demanding action to stop the rainforest fires, and they have been spreading as anger over the fires and the haze mounts** (NYT; The Star).",
