  "verse": "…only you can – in accordance with your free will – load guilt onto you and bring about a distinct own destiny, in which case you can however also liberate yourselves from your guilt again and developmentally change your destiny in accordance with your own free will…",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 264",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the algorithm and it is not the data. It is the choice — by Meta's executives, whether disclosure is designed or litigated; by the appellate courts, whether a finding that internal communications were \"designed to mislead the public\" is a fact that binds or a number that can be negotiated; by Congress, whether a federal privacy statute replaces fifty state experiments; and by every other attorney general, whether the New Mexico file becomes a template or a one-off. §28 does not say that a corporation is a person with a conscience; it says that guilt is self-loaded by free will and can be set aside only by a deliberate change of conduct — which is the one remedy no fine can purchase. Whether the next decade of platform governance is a schedule of licensing fees paid after the fact, or a disclosure regime with enforceable duties, is not fixed by the technology; it is a decision held by named judges, named legislators and named executives, and one that a different choice could still make."
